  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nConfidence remains high that dry and cool conditions will\nprevail through at least Saturday afternoon under high pressure\nand northwest flow. Temperatures will return to seasonal\naverages, so the 40s we saw this morning across parts of the \nregion won't last long. \n\nThere is the potential for a few isolated pop up showers and \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon on the leading edge of warm, \nmoist advection as flow shifts from the southwest. Brief periods\nof lightning would be the main hazard.\n\nThe threat for severe thunderstorms is expected to return early\nnext week as a ridge axis strengthens over the central CONUS and\na large-scale low intensifies north of Ontario. The evolving\nsynoptic pattern favors upstream mesoscale convective system\n(MCS) development upstream over the northern Great Lakes, with\nsubsequent propagation into the region or redevelopment along \nremnant outflow boundaries and residual convective features. \n\nIf a mesoscale convective systems (MCSs) does develop and track\ninto our region, forecast soundings indicate an environment \ncapable of supporting all severe weather hazards, including \nhail, damaging wind gusts, tornadoes, and heavy rainfall.\nHowever, the potential for large hail may be limited by a warm \nfreezing level (above 14kft), prompting the melting of hail \nstones.\n\nNCAR Machine-learning guidance continues to indicate a 5-30% \nprobability of severe thunderstorms from Sunday through Tuesday \nacross the Ohio River Valley. Colorado State University (CSU)\nmachine learning has 5-15% chance of excessive rainfall (flash\nflooding) Sunday and Monday and shifts the potential mostly \nsouth of area by Tuesday. The eventual evolution of this threat \nwill largely depend on the amplitude of the eastern U.S. trough \nand the strength and position of the upper- level ridge, which \nwill influence the track and intensity of any MCSs.",
